    "result_text": "All tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS). The full test suite consists of the single `AcceptanceTest` (the other two files are the base class and the log-capture helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured `OWNER_CREATED` audit event, keeping each piece of logic in its proper place:\n\n1. **`Owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 a new transient accessor returning the owner's current primary identifier (presently `customerCode`). This is the single source of truth the spec's forward-looking hint calls for: when the customer code is later unified into the member id, only this one method changes and every consumer \u2014 including the audit event \u2014 follows automatically.\n\n2. **`OwnerCreatedEvent`** (`service/OwnerCreatedEvent.java`) \u2014 a new immutable `record` holding `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}`, with an `EVENT_TYPE` constant and a convenience constructor that fixes `event` to `\"OWNER_CREATED\"`. A record gives the required immutability for free.\n\n3. **`OwnerAuditor.auditCreated`** (`service/OwnerAuditor.java`) \u2014 now, in addition to the existing human-readable audit line, builds and emits the structured event as JSON on the same `AUDIT` logger. A single `AtomicLong` field provides the monotonically increasing `seq` across creates; the event's identifier value comes from `owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`, and `membershipLevel` reuses the existing `ownerMapper.resolveMembershipLevel(owner)` (computed once and shared with the audit line rather than duplicated).\n\nThe emitted event looks like:\n```\n{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\nNo existing responsibilities were disturbed \u2014 the auditor stays a thin side-effect emitter, the mapper stays the source of derived membership data, and the owner owns its identity.",
